  [3mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mC. Immediate per-record POST[0m — no buffer at all; each call builds a record and fires [38;5;153mfetch(..., {keepalive: true})[39m
  right away.
  [3mWins when[0m you want the smallest possible amount of code and there's very little log volume. [3mCosts:[0m one request per log
  line. With breadcrumbs enabled that's a dozen-plus requests per login attempt, which is punishing for the client and
  for whatever receives it. Failures silently drop.
  [3mComplexity: trivial.[0m

  [1mMy recommendation: A, the black-box recorder.[0m You asked for this specifically to debug production issues, and A puts
  essentially all of its cost on the sessions that actually broke. B is the better general-purpose telemetry pipeline,
  but you'd be paying for a lot of uninteresting successful logins to get there, and you didn't ask for analytics. C I'd
  only take if you wanted this done in twenty lines and accepted that it won't scale past a trickle.

  The denominator gap is A's one real weakness, and it's cheap to close if you care: one tiny session-summary record on
  [38;5;153mpagehide[39m (correlation ID, attempt count, outcome counts — no breadcrumbs) gives you rates without shipping the
  healthy-session detail. I'd include it.

