[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good. Design, part one — structure and data flow.

  [1mArchitecture[0m

  One new ES module, [38;5;153mlogger.js[39m, with a deliberately dependency-free core. The core knows nothing about the browser: no
  [38;5;153mwindow[39m, no [38;5;153mfetch[39m, no [38;5;153mDate.now[39m, no [38;5;153mcrypto[39m. Those arrive through an init config ([38;5;153mtransport[39m, [38;5;153mnow[39m, [38;5;153midGen[39m). A separate
  exported [38;5;153minstallBrowserHandlers(logger)[39m does the wiring to real browser globals.

  That split is the whole reason the thing is testable. With no bundler and no DOM in the test environment, a logger
  that reaches for [38;5;153mwindow[39m directly can only be tested in a browser — which means, in practice, not tested. Injecting the
  seams costs a few lines and makes every rule below verifiable in plain Node.

  This does mean [38;5;153mindex.html[39m moves to [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m imports the logger. Alternative was

